            "content": [
              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Pixel Movement**\n\nI'm focusing on two specific pixels, green (3) and yellow (4). Observing the input and output from example 1, it appears pixel 3 moved closer to pixel 4. This suggests a relationship based on distance or relative position. It seems the core of the problem might be determining the rule governing this movement, and that distance between them might be the key to understanding the rule.\n\n\n**Refining Movement Logic**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the specifics of pixel 3's movement. It seems pixel 3 always moves *towards* pixel 4. This is supported by multiple examples. Moreover, it is apparent that 3 moves a single unit in the direction towards pixel 4. Example 3 and 6 suggest that if pixel 3 moves directly onto pixel 4, pixel 4 is overwritten by pixel 3. Example 4 confirms the single-step movement toward pixel 4 if they are not collocated.\n\n\n",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
              }
            ],
            "encrypted_content": null,
